WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.950
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Modernizing
traditional workflows requires

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:04.380
building Cloud First solutions.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.470
Join us in historic
downtown Austin, Texas,

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.120
as we talk to SADA Systems
about their cloud architecture

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.856
and how they've built Atom.

00:00:10.856 --> 00:00:13.712
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:15.890
So let's talk about Atom.

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.010
What does the architecture
look like for Atom?

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:20.120
OLEG SHALYGIN: Atom's
architecture's very robust.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.790
Everything is built on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.130
We're leveraging
Kubernetes engine

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.020
for all of the
different microservices,

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:31.140
and there's a various array
of portals within Atom.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.970
Each portal may have three
or four various types

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:38.310
of microservices that
back that infrastructure.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.540
It gives us the
flexibility of specifying

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:46.350
the right tool for the job,
which means we can say, hey,

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:51.650
this data is very relational,
very specific in the way

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:52.490
that it's laid out.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:55.940
We can back that with
a CloudSQL database.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.330
Whereas other data within
Atom is very non-relational

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:03.050
and dynamic based on
whoever the client may be

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:04.400
and what asset class they have.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.440
So we can specify that
database to be a NoSQL solution

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.400
hosted on MongoDB Atlas.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.130
Specifically, we
have dataflow jobs

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:21.110
where we pull data in from all
various DOTs that we work with

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:26.210
or different agencies, and
they have data today in there

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.640
as a reserver or
the ArcGIS server

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.540
that we pull from and then
real time display within Atom.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.390
And so that whole
experience is more or less

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:37.960
seamless through
Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.460
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So what other
tools inside of Google Cloud

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:41.570
are you using?

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:44.300
OLEG SHALYGIN: So we use almost
the full gamut, specifically

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:49.190
around logging, tracing, a lot
towards developer productivity,

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:53.060
so that we can be
agile enough to respond

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.040
to the demands of the customer.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.910
So we're pushing
code very regularly

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.220
on a weekly, almost
bi-weekly, basis.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.620
And for us to be
able to do that, we

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.470
need the platform to do a
lot of the heavy lifting

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:10.400
in terms of automation,
in terms of security

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:15.260
best practices, the Command
Center, threat detection,

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:15.890
you name it.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:19.430
We're leveraging the tools
to help boost developer

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:20.420
productivity.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:24.950
And that's our big win on
Atom, is enabling everybody

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:27.500
on the team to be
comfortable with the platform

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:31.062
and proficient at
provisioning these services.

00:02:31.062 --> 00:02:33.020
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah,
you talked a little bit

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.600
about Kubernetes being
kind of the cornerstone

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:36.890
of a lot of this architecture.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:38.360
Why Kubernetes?

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.670
OLEG SHALYGIN: So Kubernetes
gives us the availability

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:44.840
to scale based on demand, both
horizontally and vertically.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:49.430
So we're not running
hundreds of hundreds of VMs

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:52.070
at 10% utilization.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:55.500
We're able to utilize our
resources very effectively,

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.610
which ties directly
into the pricing models

00:02:58.610 --> 00:02:59.740
that we offer these DOTs.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:01.680
We can be very
competitive because we

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.590
are optimizing our consumption.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:11.120
And so where that plays a big
role is we have periods of time

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.790
throughout the day where we
have a high amount of traffic

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:18.590
and load, specifically around
our download and offline

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:19.220
consumption.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:22.010
So in the traditional
sense, somebody

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.530
will go out and perform
a bridge inspection.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.380
Now, before they go
out, they realize, hey,

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:32.250
I'm not going to have cell
connectivity in this location.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:34.400
So what they do is they
download everything

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.610
that they need to do on
the job, site and then

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.200
they work fully offline.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.220
So when they regain
connectivity,

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:45.840
obviously, all of those updates
come back to the application.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.470
We have a robust pipeline with
Pub/Sub and what have you,

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:54.710
but the actual
services themselves

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:57.410
need to scale very
rapidly to that demand.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:59.720
Because, as you can
imagine, the end of the day

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.530
is when all of the inspectors
come back to their hotel rooms,

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.910
they regain
connectivity, and it's

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.790
eight plus hours worth
of work now getting

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:09.780
pushed over to our application.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:13.790
So the ability to
respond to that demand

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:15.710
has been very
critical, and that's

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:20.893
the primary reason why we use
Kubernetes engine for that.

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:22.310
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So you mentioned

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:23.770
developer productivity earlier.

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:26.360
Has Google Cloud helped
you with making sure

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.610
that your developers are
more productive while working

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:29.480
on Atom?

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.397
OLEG SHALYGIN: Yeah, so
developer productivity

00:04:31.397 --> 00:04:34.350
has drastically increased since
we've adopted Google Cloud

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:37.980
Platform and really started
leveraging the full gamut.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:42.950
We can tie-in resources,
such as logging and tracing,

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:44.870
into these intuitive
dashboards that

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.720
can give a holistic
view of the application

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.370
to our development team.

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:52.280
We no longer need very
specialized resources

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.980
that specialize in
building these dashboards

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.160
or knowing some outside
tooling that they

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.390
can tie into a pipeline
or CI/CD environment.

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:06.230
Whereas, now our
developers can get back

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:11.180
to writing code quickly
and efficiently.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:15.200
And that's essentially the
gamut of what we use today,

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.140
and we continue to use things
like Cloud Run to offload

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:22.713
some of our nightly jobs,
Crons, and what have you.

00:05:22.713 --> 00:05:24.380
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
it sounds like Atom

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:25.760
was built with a
very Cloud-First

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:26.740
Architecture in mind.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:28.490
When you are implementing
this, were there

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.870
any things that you wish were
different in Google Cloud?

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:34.370
OLEG SHALYGIN: Yeah, so when
we first started the product,

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:36.450
Identity Platform
wasn't a thing.

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.090
And now it is.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.662
Ideally, we would like
to encompass everything

00:05:40.662 --> 00:05:41.870
within Google Cloud Platform.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:43.790
It gives us true visibility.

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:47.540
We don't need to go to numerous
systems and applications

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:51.230
to see where we stand
in terms of identity.

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:55.370
It would be ideal if it was
all within one platform, which

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:57.950
is what our goal has been.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:02.090
So we use Okta today
and, ideally, we'd

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:03.510
be leveraging Identity Platform.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.510
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well,
thank you so much, Oleg,

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:07.700
for coming in and telling
us about how SADA built

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:08.990
these solutions in the cloud.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:10.453
OLEG SHALYGIN: Thank you.

00:06:10.453 --> 00:06:12.120
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Thanks for joining us,

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:14.680
and stay tuned for more customer
interviews on Stackchat.

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

